Other devices used with NAHPU in the field:

- iPhone 13 mini (field expeditions in Mexico)

{% aside type="note" title="Product names and endorsements" %}

This page lists product names and models only as examples from fieldwork
experience. These mentions are not endorsements or recommendations by NAHPU.
Evaluate each device for your own needs, budget, and field conditions.

{% /aside %}

## Check the recording device

- Use a phone, tablet, or computer running a supported NAHPU build.
- Rehearse real data entry at its screen size, including long forms, camera or
  file attachments, and GPS capture if needed.
- Verify that keyboards, barcode scanners, printers, and external drives work
  through the device's actual connectors and permissions.

## Measure storage and power needs

- **Storage**: build a small practice dataset with realistic media sizes, then
  measure a project export and a full database backup. Leave headroom for
  working files and multiple retained backups rather than assuming a fixed
  drive size is enough.
- **Power**: measure battery drain during a realistic session (screen
  brightness, photography, GPS capture, and exports) and test charging with the
  exact cables, adapters, and power banks you will carry. Do not estimate
  runtime from another team's device or assume a connector type determines
  charging speed.

## Verify the backup route

Export a practice project, copy the file to your backup storage, and import it
on a separate test installation. Open the records and media there to confirm
they load. Keep at least one verified copy off the recording device—a second
folder on the same device does not protect against hardware loss.

## Prepare for the field

- Install required taxa, user configs, presets, fonts, and reference files
  before departure.
- Test the entire workflow offline, including any custom map layers.
- Choose cases and weather protection suited to transport and field conditions.

See [App Setup](/en/best-practices/app-setup/) for pre-trip rehearsal and
[Device Choice and Care](/en/best-practices/device-care/) for field routines.
